---
run: round-trip
fixpoint: executed
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-local-at-toplevel-of-sourced-file — QUESTION: `local x=1` outside any function, at
# the top level of a sourced file. Error? Fatal? And does the assignment reach the caller?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it: the dialect is "POSIX + `local`" (`dialect-quality-law`), which
# makes `local` the one non-POSIX word Dorc asks authors to rely on — and its behaviour outside a
# function is unspecified by every party.
#
# ANSWERED, and this one is a REAL semantic disagreement, not a status. Gate-9 has nowhere to commit
# a disagreement, so the measurement lives here and the case carries NO `expected.emitted` section;
# re-arming it is one line, and it would go red for as long as the two shells mean different things.
#
# measured-divergence: (mise run test:floor -- floor30-atlas, dash 0.5.12 ∩ posh 0.14.1, WSL)
#   dash: file-before-local
#         book: contained status=[2]
#         book: x-after-subshell=[outer]
#         file-before-local
#   posh: file-before-local
#         file-after-local x=[1]
#         sub: after-dot x=[1]
#         book: x-after-subshell=[outer]
#         file-before-local
#         file-after-local x=[1]
#         book: bare-survived x=[1]
#   MEANING: dash ABORTS the sourced file at `local` (status 2, and in the bare cell it takes the
#   script — `book: bare-survived` never prints); posh ACCEPTS `local` outside a function, and its
#   assignment survives into the sourcer, so `x` is 1 for everything after the `.`. That is one
#   authored line with two meanings, so `local` outside a function is OUTSIDE the base dialect —
#   which makes the paste question moot: the dialect refuses the file rather than choosing how to
#   inline it. Note both shells agree that the SUBSHELL cell leaves the book's own `x` alone, so the
#   divergence is about the file, not about scoping.
x=outer
( . ./localer.sh; printf 'sub: after-dot x=[%s]\n' "$x" ) || printf 'book: contained status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'book: x-after-subshell=[%s]\n' "$x"
. ./localer.sh
printf 'book: bare-survived x=[%s]\n' "$x"

-- localer.sh --
printf 'file-before-local\n'
local x=1
printf 'file-after-local x=[%s]\n' "$x"

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=bea255303b145b0f5d6eb66afc2e4785acf2296cd88482b5b957906a43cce8f3 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:6 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

